  "body": "Reported by the maintainer: the starter tool catalog is compiled into the application instead of being data.\n\n## Today\n- `crates/tools/src/tools.rs:456`, `pub fn catalog() -> Vec<(&'static str, Tool)>`, builds every starter tool in Rust (an `em(...)` helper per end mill and so on). Adding, renaming or tuning a tool needs a code change and a rebuild.\n- About 26 non-test call sites use `catalog()` (the tool library pane, `tool_gate::catalog_suggestion`, MCP `mcp/tools.rs`, `script/api.rs`, `commands/tool_import.rs`, ...), and many tests pick tools by catalog name (`catalog_tool(\"...\")`, e.g. `TD-1732-60`).\n- A JSON tool format already exists: `crates/app/src/tool_library.rs` (#300), the `camcraft-tools/1` envelope of `{name, definition}` using `project::tool_to_json` / `tool_from_json`.\n\n## Wanted\n- Move the catalog to an asset, e.g. `assets/tools/catalog.json`, in the existing `camcraft-tools/1` format (one tool format, not a second), embedded with `include_str!` the way `assets/posts/*.cps` and `assets/machines/*.mch` are, so the binary still works with no files next to it.\n- Parse it with the strict `parse_tool_library` reader. A malformed or invalid entry is a named error, never a panic (Prime Directive), and a test asserts the shipped asset parses cleanly with every tool valid.\n- Keep `catalog()`'s contract (names, order = display order, values) so the call sites and the tests that pick tools by name don't change. A test pins that the JSON catalog equals today's Rust catalog tool for tool before the Rust table is deleted.\n- Layering: `camcraft-tools` can't depend on `app`, where `tool_from_json` lives today. Either move the tool (de)serialisation into `camcraft-tools`, or load the asset in `app` and pass it down. Choose one and record it in `docs/decisions/`.\n- Optional, as a follow-up: also read a user catalog override, a `tools/` folder beside the user's `machines/`, the same way the fixture library and machine library find theirs.\n\n## Done means\n- `assets/tools/catalog.json` exists and is the only source of the starter catalog; no tool values remain hard-coded in `tools.rs`.\n- The equality test (JSON matches the old catalog) and the \"asset parses, every tool valid\" test pass; existing suites that use catalog names pass unchanged.\n- A bad asset entry yields a named error in a unit test, not a panic.\n",
